# Glucose Distribution

The Glucose Distribution report answers the question "how much time do I spend
in each glucose range?". It also shows how that changes with the hour of the
day.

<Video id="glucose-distribution" />

## What it shows

### The key figures

The strip at the top shows the **Mean**, **Median** and **Std Dev** (standard
deviation) of your glucose, and the number of **Readings** in the period.

### Distribution Chart

The ring shows how your time divides across the glucose ranges. The number in
the middle is your time in range, 70 to 180 mg/dL. The key under the ring names
each part.

| Range | Glucose |
|-------|---------|
| Very Low | Below 54 mg/dL |
| Low | 54 to 70 mg/dL |
| Tight Range | 70 to 140 mg/dL |
| In Range | 140 to 180 mg/dL when the tight range shows; 70 to 180 mg/dL when it does not |
| High | 180 to 250 mg/dL |
| Very High | Above 250 mg/dL |

**Distribution Statistics** gives the same values as a table, with one decimal
place.

### Hourly Distribution

Each column is one hour of the day. The column is split by range, so you can
see which hours spend more time high or low. The key names each range, with its
glucose values.

### GMI, Glycemic Variability and Fluctuation

- **GMI** (glucose management indicator) estimates your HbA1c from your average
  glucose. If the GMI is not available, the card shows your eHbA1c.
- **Glycemic Variability** shows the **GVI** (glycemic variability index) and
  **PGS** (patient glycemic status).
- **Fluctuation** shows the **Mean Total Daily Change** and the **Time in
  Fluctuation**.

The app shows glucose values in mmol/L if you chose mmol/L.

## How to use it

To see your whole target range as one part of the ring:

1. In the **Distribution Chart** card, select **Hide Tight Range**.
2. The ring now shows one **In Range** part for 70 to 180 mg/dL.
3. To split it again, select **Show Tight Range**.

The app keeps this choice in the page address. If you share or reload the page,
the chart shows the same view.

To change the period, select the date range at the top of the report, select a
period, and then select **Apply Filters**.

## Good to know

- If the period has no readings, the report shows **No readings in this date
  range**. Choose a longer period.
- If the GMI or eHbA1c uses fewer days than recommended, a badge in that card
  shows how many days it uses.
